<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anglo-Saxons</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Anglo-Saxon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Anglo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> were the Germanic-speaking peoples, chiefly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angles">Angles</a>, Saxons and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jutes">Jutes</a>, who settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> from the fifth century after the end of Roman rule, and their descendants. They founded kingdoms such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a>, Mercia, Northumbria and Kent, converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> from 597, and were united under the kings of Wessex in the tenth century as the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, which lasted until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norman-conquest">Norman Conquest</a> of 1066.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons.jpg" alt="Anglo-Saxons" width="320" height="447" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Eadfrith · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LindisfarneChiRiho.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
